 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 10,
   "text": "MELLIFONT ABBEY. The first Cistercian monastery in Ireland, founded in 1142, now a handsome ruin with a lavabo, where the monks washed. The roof's missing, but the washing arrangements are first-class."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 25,
   "text": "BRU NA BOINNE. The 'palace of the Boyne', a valley with three great passage tombs: Newgrange, Knowth and Dowth, built over 5,000 years ago. Newgrange is up the road to the east."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "THE SALMON OF KNOWLEDGE. In the legend, the young Fionn mac Cumhaill cooked the Salmon of Knowledge on the banks of the Boyne and burnt his thumb. He sucked it, and gained all its wisdom. Some of us get nothing from a burnt thumb but a burnt thumb."
  },
  {
   "x": 63,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "NEWGRANGE, EAST. Built around 3200 BC, so five hundred years older than the pyramids and a thousand older than Stonehenge. Mind the bus tours."
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "DUBLIN, SOUTH. About an hour's drive. On foot, rather more."
  },
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "WEST TO SLIGO. Up past the Dromahair roads and down to Benbulben. Keep Benbulben on your left."
  }
 ],
